    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p38_b14",
      "text": "Thus KL control is one way to mitigate over-optimization by shrinking the feasible policy\nset around the reference model. That safeguard can be useful, but it is not intrinsic\nto RLHF and it is no longer the only practical recipe.\nRecent large-scale LLM-RL\nimplementations already train successfully without an explicit KL penalty: Hu et al.\n[2025] show that vanilla PPO with rule-based rewards can scale reasoning performance\nwithout any KL regularization, while Yu et al. [2025] explicitly remove the KL term\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.58,
        484.1,
        525.59,
        585.63
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        1936,
        2102,
        2342
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.85,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 510,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p38_b15",
      "text": "from GRPO-style long-chain-of-thought training and argue that large divergence from the\ninitial model need not be suppressed when it supports better reasoning.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        591.67,
        523.35,
        621.46
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2366,
        2093,
        2485
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 159,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p38_b16",
      "text": "More importantly, KL control is not exactly to the point for the misspecification\nproblem studied here. If the raw proxy reward is imperfect, then maximizing that same\nproxy over a smaller KL ball is still a constrained optimization against a misspecified\nobjective. In that sense, KL regularization is a last-resort hedge: it limits how much\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        627.34,
        523.35,
        693.2
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2509,
        2093,
        2772
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 12.07,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 342,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p38_b17",
      "text": "damage the proxy can do by limiting how far the policy may move, but it does not repair\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        699.24,
        523.59,
        711.07
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2796,
        2094,
        2844
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 87,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p38_b18",
      "text": "the proxy itself. Our viewpoint is that a more direct mitigation is to improve the reward\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        717.12,
        523.35,
        729.01
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        2868,
        2093,
        2916
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.9,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 89,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p38_b19",
      "text": "signal being optimized. Once a robust correction is added to the proxy reward, optimizing\nthe corrected surrogate over a given feasible region is more natural than preserving the\n",
